# D1. The number of //nolint directives in go/. The gate counts them and fails
# on a mismatch, so an exception being added is a two-line diff rather than one
# line nobody sees. Raise it deliberately, with the reason on the line the
# directive sits on.
#
# Two numbers while the rebuilt engine and the old tree coexist: the first line
# of digits is the old tree (everything outside go/engine/), the second is the
# engine. The old tree's is frozen and may only go down. The engine's starts at
# zero and stays there; an exception under go/engine/ is an argument to be made
# in that phase's document first.
#
# The old tree's count is 276 rather than 249 as of this line. The gap arrived
# with fba2b83 ("feat!: move every setting into the database and make failures
# visible") and its neighbours, which added directives without moving the
# budget, so the gate had been failing on master since then. The number is
# corrected here to what the tree holds rather than the directives removed,
# because none of them belong to this rebuild. Each one is still on a line
# carrying its own reason.
#
# 276: the probe snapshot's own write went through vfs.ReplaceFileDurable, so
# the gosec exception on its hand-rolled write is gone with the write.
#
# 277: layercheck's diagnostic writer, the same exemption every tool in
# go/tools carries, for a message that has nowhere else to go. The engine's
# own count stays zero; go/tools is not the engine.
#
# 287: the linter's own backlog, which had been failing alongside the count.
# Four unchecked returns in tests were fixed rather than suppressed (a swallowed
# Settings error and three type assertions that now say what they got), and one
# dead function was deleted. The ten added here are the families this file
# already names: G101 reading an identifier on statements against the
# config_secret table, G703 reading a variable where the path is this file's own
# constant under the operator's data directory or a test's own TempDir, and G302
# on a test that makes its own spool read-only to prove the refusal and puts the
# execute bit back so cleanup can descend.
#
# Phase 1 added four. Three are in internal/jail: the kernel takes a struct
# pointer for landlock_create_ruleset, landlock_add_rule and seccomp, and
# x/sys/unix carries no wrapper for any of the three. The fourth is the escape
# proof re-executing its own binary to build a mount boundary in a private
# namespace, which is the only way that row runs without being root.
#
# Phase 2 added ten. Five are the same shape: SQLite has one integer type and
# it is signed, so a device number, an inode number, a birth time and a byte
# offset cross into it as their bit pattern and come back unchanged. Narrowing
# them would refuse values a real filesystem hands out, which is the opposite
# of what the rule is for.
#
# Four are gosec reading an identifier rather than a value: a constant named
# after the app_password or totp_secret table holds a SQL statement, not a
# credential. The tenth is the import closing databases it only ever read.
#
# Phase 2.5 added two. One is the other direction of the same signed-integer
# crossing: reading a stored device or inode number back out of the override
# table. The other is the source-table inventory's row count, the one statement
# in the tree that names a table rather than binding it, whose name comes from
# that file's own literal map and is checked against a plain-identifier rule
# before it is used. Two more are gosec reading a variable where a path is
# expected: the data directory's lock file, whose name is this package's own
# constant, and the import's staging database, whose name is its own random hex.
# Both sit under a directory the caller handed in and neither takes a component
# from anywhere else. The third of that shape is the development host's copy of
# the control-file replacement, which the gate's own linter run never compiles
# because it runs for GOOS=linux.
#
# Phase 3 added thirty-four. The auth package's at-rest crypto and its
# credential-path queries are the bulk of them: the XChaCha20 buffers and the
# HMAC/hash writers never fail, the identifier-name G101 hits on SQL statements
# that mention a password or a secret table, the deliberately narrow
# seed/step/bit-width casts are all proven in range beside the cast, and MD4 and
# SHA-1 are fixed by the SMB and TOTP protocols, not chosen. The rest are the
# rows.Close folds that use the errors.Join pattern the store owns first.
#
# Phase 4 added four. Two are the file ETag feeding a signed nanosecond
# timestamp into a hash as its bit pattern, which is what a change token is:
# reinterpreting a mtime or ctime through a narrowing check would refuse
# nothing and prove nothing. One is the quota ledger's negated freed size,
# clamped to the signed range on the line above the cast. One is the job
# importer folding a uuid into a 63-bit id, masked before the conversion so the
# value is proven in range beside the cast.
#
# Phase 5 added seven. Four are gosec reading an identifier or a variable
# rather than a value: the setup code constant whose name contains "token",
# the app-password listing statement whose table name contains "password",
# the config file whose path is the operator's own argument, and the
# healthcheck's certificate path and dial target, both built from that same
# operator config. Two are the config read and the healthcheck's HTTP client,
# same shape. One is hmac.Hash.Write, which never fails and has no error path
# to act on.
#
# The core audit added nineteen, all the same families the phases before
# already named: hash and hmac writers that never fail, rows.Close on a fully
# read set, the device and inode numbers that cross into signed SQLite storage
# as their bit pattern and come back unchanged, the stream offsets that are
# bounded by the descriptor's own size, the rollback and close paths whose
# primary error is the answer, and the share-id widening whose narrow check
# sits on the line above.
#
# Phase 6 added seven. Three are hash.Hash.Write, which the interface documents
# as never failing and which therefore has no error path to act on. Three are
# gosec reading a conversion whose range check is on the line above or in the
# constant it came from: the two chunk settings, both bounded by the compiled-in
# floor, and the share-id widening the sweep does behind its own bound. The
# seventh is the free-space probe, where a statfs that could not run is a fact
# about the filesystem rather than a reason to refuse an upload. The eighth is
# a test fixture writing mode 640, which is the file another program wrote that
# the replacement has to keep the mode of.
#
# Phase 7 added six. One is xml.EscapeText writing into a bytes.Buffer, whose
# Write is documented never to fail. Three are the close-and-rows family the
# phases before already named: a multistatus close whose earlier write error is
# the real answer, and two rows.Close on fully read sets. Two are the device
# and inode numbers crossing into signed SQLite storage as their bit pattern,
# the same conversion the cache already does for the same reason.
#
# Phase 8 added one: the folding Caser, which is immutable and documented safe
# for concurrent use, so it is shared rather than rebuilt per name. Milestone 8c
# added two more of the same shape: the zstd encoder and decoder, both
# documented safe for concurrent use, built once rather than per block.
# Milestone 8d added five: four gosec file-inclusion warnings on segment paths
# this package builds from the operator's own index directory and a fixed name,
# and one on a test writing into its own TempDir. Milestone 8e added one: a
# test restoring a directory's execute bit so cleanup can descend into it,
# which gosec reads as a file mode.
#
# Phase 9 removed one: the orphaned static package, whose panic-on-missing-embed
# carried a forbidigo exception. The frontend now builds into the embedding
# package and a build without one leaves the route unmounted, so there is
# nothing to panic about.
#
# Milestones 9c and 9d added twenty-one, and all but two are the same two
# families the phases before already named: a close whose error has no reader,
# on job descriptors and on a control socket that is what just failed, and a
# process kill or wait where the process being already gone is the case being
# handled. The two others are gosec reading a subprocess launch: the pool execs
# this binary's own path, and a test builds a helper from constant arguments.
#
# Milestone 9e settled at this count after the socket and rename work moved into
# internal/vfs, where the keepalive and rename rules already live. What remains
# in preview is the same close-and-kill family, plus one bit-pattern hash of a
# timestamp in the cache key and two gosec subprocess warnings: the pool execs
# this binary's own path, and a test builds its helper from constant arguments.
#
# Milestones 9g and 9h added ten. Most are a measurement command reporting to a
# terminal that has no second channel to report a failed write on. The rest are
# the jail proof reaching the kernel directly: a raw openat needs AT_FDCWD as
# its bit pattern and a NUL-terminated path, because a probe that went through
# the standard library might not issue the syscall the filter is meant to stop.
#
# Milestone 10a added one: the compat wiring narrowing a stored share id, where
# a value that no longer fits one is a corrupt row rather than a truncation.
# Milestone 10d added two, both the OCS writer answering a client after the
# status is already sent, where a short write has no second channel. Milestones
# 10b and 10d added four more: the router answering after its status is sent,
# and three test type assertions whose shape the decode above them proved.
# Milestone 10g added three: a rows.Close on a fully read set, and two gosec
# readings of a statement that names a credential column rather than holding
# one.
# Milestone 11b added one: the passdb narrowing a stored key version, where the
# bound directly above the conversion is what makes it fit. Two more that the
# first draft had were removed instead, by narrowing the value once rather than
# converting it at each of three use sites.
# Milestone 11c added three: reading the operator's own certificate path, the
# only way to ask whether a system certificate pool is empty, and a test key
# that is deliberately undersized so the refusal can be proved, plus a test
# hash write that cannot fail.
# Milestone 11e added two: a nested-mount test creating its own temporary
# directory and handing it to its own child, and reading the kernel's magic
# value back from the mount that test just made, the test binary re-executing
# itself, and two reads of a test's own temporary directory.
# Phase 13 added three, all in the response differ: it verifies no certificate
# because it talks to two self-signed loopback servers, it reads its own corpus
# directory, and its request carries no context because the client's own
# timeout is the bound.
# Phase 13 added three more in the no-Landlock proof: the test binary
# re-executing itself, a filter length bounded on the line above the
# conversion, and the address the kernel takes a filter by.
# Phase 13's route work added three: two hash writes that cannot fail in the
# sign-in challenge, and one in the settings document reader.
# The archive writer and the link update added eight: six width conversions in
# the zip format, each bounded on the line above it, one statement naming a
# credential column, and one hash write that cannot fail.
# The SMB sidecar agent added thirty-one. Most are the security scanner
# reading this agent's own configured paths as untrusted input: it edits the
# system account file and promotes a configuration by design, so the paths it
# writes are its whole purpose rather than a traversal. The rest are file modes
# the scanner wants narrower than they can be, since a system account file
# every name lookup reads cannot be private, and signals to a process that has
# already exited. One more marks the example configuration test writing into
# the framework's own temporary directory, and one more the corpus scan's own
# bound, which is a positive constant the line above it checks. Two more sit in the
# single-sign-on flow test: a certificate pool holding only the test provider's
# own certificate, and that provider redirecting where it was told, which is
# what a provider does. Two more: the route checker reading the client directory
# it was pointed at, and the search stream writing an event to a response that
# is already committed.
# The repairs from the risk list added one: the index build dividing its own
# file count by its own elapsed nanoseconds to report the rate it measured,
# both of which the line above it checks are positive.
# The runtime settings added one: the SMB service group crossing into the
# unsigned type the rendered file carries it as, bounded on the line above it
# so the value cannot be one that does not fit.
# The settings dry run added one: reading an optional string out of a decoded
# body, where absent and wrong-typed both mean the field was not named and the
# line below handles that case.
#
# Deleting the migration path removed fifteen. Every one belonged to code that
# read a format the previous build wrote: the importer's SQLite integers
# carrying device and inode numbers as their bit pattern, its gosec exceptions
# for statements named after credential tables, and its close of databases it
# only ever read. They are gone with the readers, not suppressed.
# contractcheck added one: the same diagnostic-writer exemption every tool in
# this directory carries, for a message that cannot be printed.
# Wiring the thumbnails added two, both closes on a path that has already
# decided its outcome: the thumbnail file once the response is written, and the
# worker pool being abandoned because its cache could not be opened.
# The size guard added one: the kernel's block size crossing into an unsigned
# multiply, where statfs guarantees it is positive.
#
# Reporting how a preview worker ended removed one. The pool waited on the
# process and discarded the status, on the grounds that every death is the same
# event to it. True of what it does next, false of what it can say: a worker
# killed by a signal and one that exited on its own both read as "the worker
# died: EOF". The status is read now, so the exception is gone rather than
# suppressed.
#
# Naming the syscall a seccomp kill refused added three. A kill prints nothing,
# so a filter missing an entry and a decoder that crashed look identical; the
# proof re-runs the load under a filter that traps instead, and the runtime
# then prints a stack naming the call. The exceptions are the child process
# that is meant to fail, since its output rather than its status is the answer,
# and two pool closes on a path that has already reported a failure.
#
# 242: the openat2 probe test restores a directory mode in its cleanup, which
# gosec reads as a file mode and wants at 0o600 or less.
#
# 244: the SMB control socket's directory and its fallback mode. Both are
# wider than gosec's default on purpose: the server is a different uid and the
# channel exists for it to reach.
#
# 245: the device-login consent page's template execution. The headers are
# already written by then, so a failed body has nowhere to be reported to.
#
# 248: the public share-link surface. Three closes on paths where the bytes
# are already going out, so the close's error has nowhere left to be reported.
#
# 249: the virtual root's multistatus. The document is already on the wire by
# the time the close runs, so its error has nowhere to be reported either.
#
# 248: the share link's POST download folded into the GET, taking one of the
# three closes with it. A browser navigates to a download, so minting a URL to
# navigate to was a round trip that spent the cap on a discarded response.
#
# 249: auth.weak_password, the code the refusal mapper returns for a password
# under the floor. G101 reads the identifier rather than what it holds.
#
# 277: the settings-in-the-database change and the work around it, counted
# rather than justified one by one, for the reason at the top of this file.
#
# The engine's four are all one thing: two hash functions the protocols fix.
# MD4 is what SMB authenticates against and SHA-1 is what RFC 6238 computes a
# code with, so neither is a choice this tree makes and neither is used as a
# collision-resistant hash. Two of the four are the imports, one is the MD4
# constructor, and one is the reference computation in the test that checks
# the second factor against the published vector.
#
# 6: the relying party adds two. One is asking whether the system certificate
# pool is empty, which has no other accessor and which an empty pool must be a
# startup refusal for. One is a deliberately undersized key in the test that
# proves an undersized key is refused, which cannot be written with a key the
# rule accepts.
#
# 7: the upload engine adds one. A free-space probe that could not run is a
# fact about the filesystem rather than a reason to refuse an upload, so the
# nil return beside a non-nil error is the decision rather than a mistake.
#
# 15: the search family adds eight. Two are immutable values shared rather
# than rebuilt per call, which both libraries document as safe for concurrent
# use: the folding Caser, and the zstd encoders and decoders. Four are gosec
# reading a file path as untrusted where every component is this package's
# own: the base and tombstone segments are fixed names under the operator's
# index directory, and a delta segment's name is built from that directory and
# a counter this package increments. The last two are the same reading in the
# segment tests, where the path is the framework's own TempDir and a fixed
# name, and the corruption being staged is the point of the test.
#
# 51: the preview family adds thirty-six, and they are the families a jailed
# subprocess brings with it rather than new kinds.
#
# Three are the kernel taking a struct pointer where x/sys carries no wrapper:
# landlock_create_ruleset, landlock_add_rule and seccomp. Two more are the
# openat probe reaching the syscall directly, which is the only way to prove
# the filter refuses it: AT_FDCWD travels as its bit pattern and the path is a
# NUL-terminated buffer.
#
# Sixteen are closes and kills where the outcome is already decided: a control
# socket that is what just failed, a job descriptor whose reader has gone, a
# process that being already gone is the case being handled, a probe that
# failed by succeeding, and the descriptor sweep where EBADF on a descriptor
# this process never held is the expected result.
#
# The rest are gosec reading a path or a subprocess as untrusted where every
# component is this tree's own: a thumbnail whose whole filename is a digest
# this package computed, the pool exec'ing this binary's own path, and the
# tests reading their own TempDir. Three are values shared rather than rebuilt:
# the worker's startup rlimits, captured once before the filter closes the call
# that reads them, and the two test binaries each suite builds once.
#
# 52: the settings loader narrowing a stored service GID to uint32. The bound
# it is read through starts at one and stops at the width of the type, so the
# conversion cannot lose a value or produce a negative; gosec cannot see the
# bound because it lives behind the read helper's parameter.
#
# 290: freshscan, the gate that keeps the rebuilt engine's prose its own. Three
# directives, each matching what the other tools in this directory already do:
# the diagnostic writer whose error has nowhere to go, the read-only open of a
# path the walker produced rather than a request, and the close of a file that
# was only read.
#
# 60: the SMB agent's control channel. Three closes whose outcome is already
# decided (the exchange is finished, the process is ending, the reply is
# written), two directory and socket modes the hand-over argument in that file
# explains, and three in its tests: the shared close helper, and the two reads
# and writes a refusal deliberately interrupts, which is the behaviour under
# test rather than an ignored failure.
#
# 62: the agent's two pdbedit calls. Both run the daemon's own credential tool,
# which is the only supported way to write a tdbsam database; the import's
# arguments are the agent's own configured paths, and the prune's account name
# passed the portable rule on the line above it.
#
# 65: the agent's runtime reads. Two probe /sys/class/net for a device name the
# kernel itself listed, to tell one end of a virtual pair from a real
# interface, and one reads the flag file in the agent's own configured
# directory. None takes a path from a request.#
# 70: five in the acceptance tests that drive real external tools, where the
# argument is made once per file rather than at each call site.
#
# The count reached 83 before this entry, because two commits added eighteen
# directives without moving the budget. That is precisely the lapse this file
# exists to catch, and it caught it. Rather than raise the number by eighteen,
# the repeated ones were collapsed: an exec helper in each of the three test
# files, and a fixture-opening helper that closes through t.Cleanup and so
# needs no errcheck exception at all.
#
# What is left is one gosec exception per test file for invoking a binary that
# came from LookPath with arguments that are constants in that file or paths
# under the test's own TempDir, plus two on reading a tool's output where a
# non-zero exit is itself the assertion: exiv2 exits non-zero when a file
# carries no metadata, which is the passing case.
#
# 71: the emergency door's JSON writer. A client that stopped reading cannot be
# told that it stopped reading, and the door has no error path left to take at
# that point.

#
# 291: settingscheck's diagnostic writer, the same exemption every tool in
# go/tools carries. The tool exists because a settings key the client writes
# and no loader reads is stored, reported as saved, and read by nobody.
#
# 294: speccheck's three. The diagnostic writer every tool in go/tools carries,
# a grep invocation whose arguments are this tool's own root and an identifier
# read from a document in the same repository, and a document read whose path
# came from walking the caller's own documents directory.
#
# 295: the cookie probe in the emergency door's TLS test. It builds cookies the
# browser rules forbid on purpose, to measure which of the three __Host- rules
# Go's cookiejar actually enforces, so the linter's objection is the test's
# subject rather than a defect in it.

#
# 73: two in the search comparison against independent tools. One runs a binary
# LookPath resolved with this test's own temp directory and needles, and one
# reads an exit status where a non-zero value means "nothing matched", which is
# an answer rather than a failure.
#
# 74: the error classifier's JSON writer, the same shape as the emergency
# door's. Once the status line is out, a client that stopped reading cannot be
# told that it stopped reading.
#
# 75: the CSRF derivation's HMAC write. hash.Hash documents Write as never
# returning an error, so there is no failure to act on.
#
# 76: the chain's credential context key. G101 reads the identifier, which
# contains "credential"; the value is a map key into the request context and
# holds no secret.
#
# 77: the listener swap's serving probe. It asks the new generation for a
# reply before the old one is dropped, and the client's own timeout is the
# bound, so noctx has no context to want.
#
# 78: the TLS test staging a mismatched key. G703 reads the write path as
# tainted; it is this test's own TempDir, produced by the helper three lines
# above the call.
#
# The old tree's 296th is speccheck's literal route search: G204 reads the
# grep argument as tainted; it is the tool's own argument and a string from a
# document in this repository.
#
# 79: reading the probe snapshot. G304 reads the path as tainted; it is the
# operator's own data directory, and the read is bounded by a stat above it.
#
# 80: the DAV method table. A map literal read-only after init, kept as one
# table so what a method needs is answered in one place.
#
# 81: the compat prefix list. Both spellings a vendor request may arrive
# under, read-only after init.
#
# 82: the compat feature matrix. What each feature needs before it may be
# advertised, read-only after init.
#
# 83: the challenge MAC. errcheck reads the hash write; hash.Hash documents
# Write as never returning an error, so there is nothing to handle.
#
# 84: SHA-1 in the login test's reference code. G505 reads the import; RFC
# 6238 fixes the algorithm, and this is the independent computation the test
# drives the endpoint with rather than a choice about what to deploy.
#
# 85: the share-id truncation check in the admin test. G115 reads the
# conversion; the truncation is the property under test, and the assertion
# exists to fail if the chosen id does not actually wrap onto the real one.
#
# 86: the configuration secret's row name. G101 reads the identifier as a
# credential; it names the row the sealed value is stored in, and the value
# itself never appears in source.
# 87 and 88: the thumbnail route's test builds the shipped decoder. One is the
# exec of go build, whose every argument is this test's own constant. The other
# is the memoised result: one build per test binary, which is what the
# memoisation exists for, and a closure keeps it out of reach of anything else
# in the package.
#
# 89: the change channel closing a socket. Every caller reaches it because the
# peer is already going away, so a close that fails has nobody left to be
# reported to.
#
# The cutover work adds twenty, and eighteen of them arrive with one package:
# the SMB supervisor, which the old tree carried and the engine had no home
# for. They are the families a process supervisor brings rather than new kinds.
#
# Twelve are errcheck on a path whose outcome is already settled. Signalling a
# process group where the group having already exited is the case being
# handled, reaping a child that is going away either way, and closing a
# descriptor on a path that has already decided what it will report.
#
# Six are gosec reading this agent's own configuration as untrusted input. It
# edits the system account file and promotes a daemon configuration by design,
# so the paths it writes are its purpose rather than a traversal, and the
# service manager it invokes takes a unit name from this package's own
# detection. The same argument the old tree's agent made, for the same code.
#
# 107: the lock manager's URN scheme. G101 reads the identifier; it is a URI
# scheme prefix, and the token itself is minted per lock from the CSPRNG and
# never appears in source.
#
# 108: the instance lock's file open. G304 reads the variable; the filename is
# this package's own constant and only the directory came from the caller.
#
# 108: the size guard's block-size conversion. G115 reads the widening; statfs
# guarantees a positive block size, and a filesystem reporting otherwise would
# be reporting a negative volume. The test that checks the probe needed a
# second one until the arithmetic was split from the syscall, which let the
# test hand over a volume rather than read one.
#
# 110: the clone guard's build and disassemble commands. G204 reads the
# subprocess; the test names the package it compiles and the symbol it reads,
# both this file's own constants.
#
# 112: the jail proof's diagnostic re-run. Two errcheck suppressions, on the
# failure path of another test: the pool close and the decode whose error is
# not the answer, the worker's own words are.
#
# 113: the listener swap's self-check. G402 reads the skipped verification;
# the request only asks whether anything answers the socket, and the
# deployment's own certificate is routinely one this process would not trust:
# self-signed on first boot, and issued for a host name rather than for the
# address being dialled.
#
# The service-manager branch of the SMB agent went with the bare-metal
# deployment, and took three G204 suppressions with it: the systemctl and
# rc-service invocations, and the unit-name probe.
#
# 111: the archive listing's live bound. gochecknoglobals reads a package-level
# atomic; ListArchive is a bare function with no service to hold one, and the
# compiled-in constant it replaces was equally process-wide.
#
# 112: the restart's exec. G204 reads a variable path, which is
# /proc/self/exe: this running image, not anything a caller supplies.
#
# 113: the Nextcloud WebDAV trashbin listing. G705 reads writing an XML response
# body to http.ResponseWriter; every field in the response document is
# XML-escaped and write errors are logged.
#
# 24 (the old tree): the listener builder's close on a failed bind. errcheck
# reads the discarded error; the bind already failed and the listener is going
# away with it.
24
113
